# Create a clear structure

People rarely read every word on a screen. They skim. Most people:

- [read only 20 to 28% of the words on a web page](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/how-little-do-users-read/), and skim even more when they are impatient
- [read in an F-shaped pattern](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/f-shaped-pattern-reading-web-content/): across the top, down the left side, then across again until they find what they need

People in the home may also be tired, half-asleep or holding the shopping. They may be glancing at a wall tablet while an alarm sounds. A clear structure helps them finish the task quickly and get back to what they were doing.

## Frontload your content

Lead with what matters most. The sooner you make your point, the more likely people are to see it.

Then move to the details. This shape is known as an “inverted pyramid”. A notification, for example, starts with what happened, “The back door has been open for 15 minutes”, then gives the detail.

<h3 id="wg-051">Say what must be true and what will change</h3>

Before a step, say what the person needs already: “You need the Home Assistant Companion app on your phone.” Before a risky step, say what it will change: “Restoring a backup replaces your current settings. Changes made since the backup are lost.” For warnings about risks to people, see [Choose urgency from evidence](states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

## Break up the text with headings

The title already uses H1, so start your headings at H2 and go no deeper than H4. Read how to [format headings](../formatting-content/text-formatting.html#wg-080).

Make headings:

- descriptive: avoid general headings such as “Introduction”
- frontloaded: put the most important words first
- active: start with a verb where you can, such as “Add a Zigbee device” rather than “You can add a Zigbee device”
- removable: the text still makes sense without them

Do not write headings that:

- are questions: a question is hard to frontload, and people came for an answer
- use technical terms you have not explained yet

A heading can sit straight under another, though a line of context often helps.

Put a main button under a heading that names its task. For example, on a dashboard, put the robot vacuum’s **Start** button under a heading such as “Clean downstairs”.

## Use bullet point lists and steps

Bulleted lists and numbered steps:

- cut repetition
- make a list quicker to scan

[Text formatting](../formatting-content/text-formatting.html#lists) explains how to format them.

<h3 id="wg-050">Order instructions around the task</h3>

Give one action per step, in the order the person does them. Put the step before the reason, and say what the person will see.

<h3 id="wg-052">Refer to the actual interface</h3>

Use the label shown on screen, in bold. Write “select”, not “click”. To add a utility room:

1. Select **Settings**, then **Areas, labels & zones**. Your areas appear.
2. Select **Create area**. A dialog opens.
3. In **Name**, enter “Utility room”.
4. Select **Create**. Utility room appears in your areas.

## Avoid footnotes

Leave out footnotes. They were made for print, not screens. Put anything important in the main text, and drop the rest.

## Do not repeat yourself

If there is a [summary](summaries.html) under the title, do not say it again in the first paragraph.

<h2 id="wg-054">Ask only what you need</h2>

Ask a question only if the answer changes what happens next. Mark optional questions with “(optional)”.

<h3 id="wg-061">Ask for personal information only when needed</h3>

Say why you need it and what happens if it is left blank: “Phone number (optional). Used only to text you when the alarm sounds. Leave it blank to get alarms in the app only.”
